<article class="env-corollary" id="0143">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0143">Corollary <span data-tag="0143">3.6.4.2</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a topological space. Then the counit map $v_{X}: | \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(X) | \rightarrow X$ is a weak homotopy equivalence of topological spaces. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      We must show that $\operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(v_ X): \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(| \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(X) | ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(X)$ is a homotopy equivalence of Kan complexes. This is clear, since $\operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(v_ X)$ is left inverse to the unit map $u_{ \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(X)}: \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(X) \rightarrow \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(| \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(X) | )$, which is a weak homotopy equivalence by virtue of Theorem <a data-tag="0142" href="/tag/0142">3.6.4.1</a> (and therefore a homotopy equivalence, since both $\operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(X)$ and $\operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }( | \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(X) | )$ are Kan complexes). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
